From b5a4a9ccea7b34e084f200a6b85e30d21cec21f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:56:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] TEMPO: water at or below r1 goes to vapour instead of
 being deleted

Each hydrometeor *_check_and_update routine, when q1d + qten*dt <= r1,
overwrote qten so the species ended the step at zero. What was left was
deleted, or created when an overshoot had left it negative. Give the
routines optional qvten, tten and lat_ocp arguments and move that amount to
vapour with its latent heat before the overwrite. Every call in tempo_main
up to the final update passes them, with latent heat over cp from the entry
state (lvap for cloud and rain, lsub for ice, snow, graupel, hail),
including the checks between semi-Lagrangian substeps. The calls after the
final update do not: qv1d and t1d are final there, and the clip exchange
(M1, first commit) has already moved everything at or below r1. Nor does the
diagnostic copy of cloud plus PBL cloud used for the effective radii. The
routines and M1 also record the vapour they take to fill a negative (qvneg)
and its heating (tneg), for the floor below.

Vapour floor. An exchange can take more vapour than its level holds (a
negative in a dry layer), and the host can pass vapour below min_qv; the
final update then clipped it at min_qv, creating water. Vapour that would
end the step below min_qv is now refilled first from the same layer's
condensate (with its latent heat, numbers scaled with mass), then from the
column's vapour, every level above min_qv giving the same fraction of its
excess, as the vapour floor of fix M1 does in Thompson. Only a column with
no vapour to spare is still floored: that water is created and counted in
tempo_main_diags%vapour_floor_water (kg m-2), and the latent heat of the
negatives it filled is taken back, so heat always matches the water that
actually changed phase. -1e-4 kg/kg of cloud in three dry layers above rain:
the previous commit deleted it at entry (0.127 kg/m2 created); version 2 of
this commit filled it from vapour that was not there (0.25 K of latent heat,
and the clip still created the water); now it is filled from the column's
vapour (0.25 K, nothing created). A column that cannot pay has every level
at or below min_qv: none of 30,880 test calls, including 3,000 random
columns, had one; a constructed one (no vapour, -1e-6 kg/kg of cloud at two
levels) creates 8.7e-4 kg/m2, counted, with no heat.

Early return (no hydrometeor above r1, not supersaturated). The routines
zero q1d in place at entry and leave the matching vapour and heat in qvten
and tten; those were dropped. They are now applied where they acted, with
the same vapour floor; elsewhere the column is returned exactly as it came.
A column with no tendencies is returned bit for bit, also where the host
passed vapour below min_qv (version 2 of this commit raised it there,
creating 1.24e-3 kg/m2 per call for -1e-6 kg/kg at three levels). A
subsaturated 10-level column with 5e-13 kg/kg of rain, -1e-8 kg/kg of cloud
at one level and traces of ice, snow and graupel at or below r1: the call
created 2.1e-6 kg/m2 of water; now 9e-16.

Semi-Lagrangian sedimentation books in its tendency the r1 floor it applies
to the transported state, which created water. That floor is now condensed
from vapour, with its latent heat (rain, graupel and hail mass; numbers and
graupel volume are not water), and the routine keeps its order. Taking the
tendency before the floor instead (version 2 of this commit) also removes
the floored mass from levels the precipitation has just left, and that mass
is what the fall-speed routines see there: a floored level holds
(r1/rho)*rho, just above or below r1 depending on rounding, and is given its
own fall speed or the one copied from above. Without it the velocity above
the precipitation top was zero and rain and graupel fell slower (two-layer
rain at 300 s delivered 0.335 instead of 0.441 kg/m2 in its first step;
graupel 39 % instead of 98 % of its total by 900 s). Now the first call's
precipitation is bit for bit the previous commit's in every semi-Lagrangian
case tested (70 single columns, each also with the initial vapour perturbed
by 1e-15 to 1e-12). Later steps start from states that differ by the
exchanged vapour and so differ by about as much as such perturbations make
the previous commit differ from itself (because of that rounding edge; on
main and v3.0.5 the mean of nine perturbed runs moves by a median 0.1 of the
previous commit's own spread over those cases, against 0.5 to 1 with version
2).

The flux-form sedimentation routine transported every level, including the
r1 and r2 placeholders the check routines leave in empty levels, into which
the fall-speed routines copy the fall speed from above. That mass was booked
as precipitation and paid for from vapour by the exchanges. It now leaves
inputs at or below limit in place (r1-sized changes only: its fall speeds
are fixed for the step).

Hail (hyperaware): after hail is split off, the graupel check runs again on
the tendencies the first run left. The first run has already applied them to
qg1d, ng1d and qb1d (and moved what it removed to vapour), so the second run
applied them twice: graupel at or below r1 was turned back into graupel
(-1e-6 kg/kg became +1e-6, 9.5e-4 kg/m2 created in one call), and the number
and volume limits were applied twice. Its tendencies now start from zero.
The split and the hail-graupel recombination also wrote ng1d and qb1d when
they are absent (out of bounds; a crash with bounds checking); they now
leave them alone.

Port of ufs-community/ccpp-physics fix M5, which does the same for Thompson
(2e-8 of the precipitation in UFS). In TEMPO's 59-level test column (closure
as in the first commit, gfortran, double precision, 20 s x 180 / 60 s x 60),
after the previous two commits the residual was 1.3e-7 / 4.3e-8 (standard)
and 1.3e-7 / 4.4e-8 (semi-Lagrangian) of the precipitation, water created at
up to 2.3e-8 kg/m2 per step; with this commit |sum(W1 - W0 + P)|/sum(P) is
at most 7.9e-15 with either sedimentation scheme and time step (largest
single step 5.8e-14 kg/m2, double-precision rounding). The hour's
precipitation against the previous commit: standard -6.8e-7 and +6.1e-5
kg/m2 at 20 and 60 s (the 60 s runs separate at the second step, as with
version 2 of this commit); semi-Lagrangian -3.6e-7 and -1.9e-3 kg/m2
(-6.3e-5), where 1e-14 to 1e-9 relative perturbations of the initial vapour
move the previous commit by -8.4e-9 to +2.8e-7 and -2.2e-3 to 0 kg/m2.

Over 30,880 one-column calls (adversarial cases: negative, tiny and r1-sized
condensate, dry and supersaturated layers, thin layers and large Courant
numbers, hail-aware on and off; and 3,000 random columns) the largest
residual is 2.0e-13 kg/m2, from 300-600 s semi-Lagrangian steps whose remap
rounds at that level (the same calls in version 2); no negative condensate
is left, and at every level the latent heat matches the condensate gained or
lost (to 1.2e-4, the temperature dependence of Lv).

AI-assisted: written with Claude Code (Anthropic); reviewed by the author.
